2009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;9.46</td><td char="." align="char">&#x0003c;&#x02009;0.0001</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Waist circumference</td><td char="." align="char">84.97&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;8.95</td><td align="left">84.97&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;8.92</td><td char="." align="char">85.38&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;8.67</td><td char="." align="char">85.61&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;8.4</td><td char="." align="char">&#x0003c;&#x02009;0.0001</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Total cholesterol</td><td char="." align="char">193.91&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;42.69</td><td align="left">194.98&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;42.65</td><td char="." align="char">194.21&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;42.43</td><td char="." align="char">191.34&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;41.84</td><td char="." align="char">&#x0003c;&#x02009;0.0001</td></tr><tr><td align="left">SBP</td><td char="." align="char">128.87&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;16.11</td><td align="left">128.91&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;15.95</td><td char="." align="char">128.99&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;15.68</td><td char="." align="char">128.67&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;15.46</td><td char="." align="char">&#x0003c;&#x02009;0.0001</td></tr><tr><td align="left">DBP</td><td char="." align="char">78.74&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;10.36</td><td align="left">79.1&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;10.33</td><td char="." align="char">79.01&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;10.23</td><td char="." align="char">78.36&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;10.1</td><td char="." align="char">&#x0003c;&#x02009;0.0001</td></tr><tr><td align="left">LDL-C</td><td char="." align="char">109.35&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;39.71</td><td align="left">109.76&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;39.9</td><td char="." align="char">109.49&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;39.68</td><td char="." align="char">108.68&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;38.75</td><td char="." align="char">&#x0003c;&#x02009;0.0001</td></tr><tr><td align="left">HDL-C</td><td char="." align="char">51.91&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;18.72</td><td align="left">52&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;18.81</td><td char="." align="char">51.44&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;19.33</td><td char="." align="char">50.93&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;18.52</td><td char="." align="char">&#x0003c;&#x02009;0.0001</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Fasting glucose</td><td char="." align="char">145.13&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;49.43</td><td align="left">146.39&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;48.51</td><td char="." align="char">144.36&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;46.48</td><td char="." align="char">140.5&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;43.01</td><td char="." align="char">&#x0003c;&#x02009;0.0001</td></tr><tr><td align="left">BMI</td><td char="." align="char">24.94&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;3.5</td><td align="left">25&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;3.53</td><td char="." align="char">25.06&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;3.42</td><td char="." align="char">24.98&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;3.23</td><td char="." align="char">&#x0003c;&#x02009;0.0001</td></tr><tr><td align="left">DM duration</td><td char="." align="char">3.51&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;3.72</td><td align="left">3.21&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;3.68</td><td char="." align="char">3.38&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;3.71</td><td char="." align="char">3.88&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;3.8</td><td char="." align="char">&#x0003c;&#x02009;0.0001</td></tr><tr><td align="left">eGFR</td><td char="." align="char">85.88&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;34.75</td><td align="left">87.42&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;36.11</td><td char="." align="char">86.31&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;35.01</td><td char="." align="char">83.83&#x02009;&#x000b1;&#x02009;36.47</td><td char="." align="char">&#x0003c;&#x02009;0.0001</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Triglyceride *</td><td char="." align="char">141.7 (141.48, 141.91)</td><td align="left">143.74 (143.51, 143.97)</td><td char="." align="char">145.01 (144.81, 145.21)</td><td char="." align="char">139.53 (139.37, 139.69)</td><td char="." align="char">&#x0003c;&#x02009;0.0001</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap>
</p></sec><sec id="Sec15"><title>Income and amputation events</title><p id="Par25">A total of 9552 diabetes-related limb amputations occurred during the follow-up period, and the incidence rate per 1000 person-years varied inversely with income. Q1&#x02009;+&#x02009;Medical Aid, the lowest-income group, exhibited the highest amputation rate (0.64 per 1000 person-years), while Q4, the highest-income group, showed the lowest rate (0.45 per 1000 person-years) (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="Tab2" ref-type="table">2</xref>). In stepwise models, adjustment for age and sex (Model 2) revealed a graded association between higher income and lower risk of amputation. Additional adjustment for lifestyle factors and comorbidities (Model 3) modestly attenuated this association, but the overall trend persisted. After further adjustment for diabetes severity markers, including diabetes duration and fasting (Model 4), hazard ratios were slightly further attenuated: the association for Q2 was no longer statistically significant (HR 0.955, 95% CI 0.898&#x02013;1.015), whereas Q3 and Q4 remained significantly associated with lower risk (e.g., Q4 HR 0.668, 95% CI 0.631&#x02013;0.706).</p><p id="Par26">
<table-wrap id="Tab2"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Income quartile and hazard ratios of the risk of amputation in patients with diabetic foot.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left">Income</th><th align="left">
<italic>N</italic>
</th><th align="left">Event</th><th align="left">Duration</th><th align="left">IR per 1,000</th><th align="left">Model 1&#x000a0;h (95% CI)</th><th align="left">Model 2&#x000a0;h (95% CI)</th><th align="left">Model 3&#x000a0;h (95% CI)</th><th align="left">Model 4&#x000a0;h (95% CI)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left">Q1&#x02009;+&#x02009;medical aid</td><td char="." align="char">541,177</td><td char="." align="char">2419</td><td char="." align="char">3775932.87</td><td char="." align="char">0.64</td><td align="left">1.00 (ref.)</td><td align="left">1.00 (ref.)</td><td align="left">1.00 (ref.)</td><td align="left">1 (ref.)</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Q2</td><td char="." align="char">489,224</td><td char="." align="char">1956</td><td char="." align="char">3460213.71</td><td char="." align="char">0.57</td><td align="left">0.881 (0.83, 0.935)</td><td align="left">0.926 (0.873&#x02013;0.983)</td><td align="left">0.933 (0.879&#x02013;0.99)</td><td align="left">0.955 (0.898, 1.015)</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Q3</td><td char="." align="char">655,489</td><td char="." align="char">2336</td><td char="." align="char">4645724.50</td><td char="." align="char">0.50</td><td align="left">0.783 (0.74, 0.829)</td><td align="left">0.785 (0.742&#x02013;0.831)</td><td align="left">0.804 (0.76&#x02013;0.851)</td><td align="left">0.82 (0.774, 0.87)</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Q4</td><td char="." align="char">894,695</td><td char="." align="char">2841</td><td char="." align="char">6257787.13</td><td char="." align="char">0.45</td><td align="left">0.709 (0.671, 0.748)</td><td align="left">0.629 (0.596&#x02013;0.664)</td><td align="left">0.662 (0.627&#x02013;0.699)</td><td align="left">0.668 (0.631, 0.706)</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p>Model 1: Non-adjusted.</p><p>Model 2: Adjusted for age and sex.</p><p>Model 3: adjusted for age and sex, smoking, alcohol, exercise, hypertension, dyslipidemia, BMI, eGFR.</p><p>Model 4: adjusted for age and sex, smoking, alcohol, exercise, hypertension, dyslipidemia, BMI, eGFR, diabetes duration, fasting glucose.</p><p>CI&#x02009;=&#x02009;confidence interval; IR&#x02009;=&#x02009;incidence rate per 1,000 person-years.</p><p>All p-values for model comparisons&#x02009;&#x0003c;&#x02009;0.0001.</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap>
</p><p id="Par27">Analyses incorporating robust standard errors, region-level frailty terms, and region fixed effects produced results consistent with the main Cox models (Supplementary Table <xref rid="MOESM1" ref-type="media">S4</xref>). The frailty variance estimate (0.00038) indicated minimal inter-regional heterogeneity, and clustering did not materially affect hazard ratio estimates.</p></sec><sec id="Sec16"><title>Stratified analysis: age, sex, and diabetes duration</title><p id="Par28">In subgroup analyses, the protective association between higher income and limb preservation was most pronounced among individuals aged&#x02009;&#x0003c;&#x02009;65 years (p for interaction&#x02009;&#x0003c;&#x02009;0.0001). (Table&#x000a0;<xref rid="Tab3" ref-type="table">3</xref>) The IR in Q4 for individuals younger than 65 years was 0.31 per 1000 person-years compared to 0.57 in Q1&#x02009;+&#x02009;Medical Aid (HR 0.586; 95% CI 0.543&#x02013;0.632). Among individuals aged&#x02009;&#x02265;&#x02009;65 years, the protective effect was weaker (Q4 HR 0.787; 95% CI 0.725&#x02013;0.854).</p><p id="Par29">
